> effect.  (Traditionally, the random generators available in C
> have been pretty bad.)

	I agree with you on a purely technical level, but I'm wondering if
it makes any practical difference.  Remember that not only are the
results of the RNG (pseudo-)random, but the purposes for which it's
called will be different for every game depending on what the player
does.  The longer you spend on a level, the more the RNG will be called
for monster generation, if nothing else.  Go down the stairs or quaff
that smoky potion first?  Whichever one you do first will get the next
call of the RNG, which in turn will have ripple effects throughout the
whole game - if a monster is generated, then equipment may have to be
generated for it, which in turn may require other calls to the RNG if it
decides to use an item, step on a trap, etc.  I just doubt that the
choice of PRNG is really going to have much visible effect unless the
current one is _seriously_ pathological.

